build        : 1cfd33433d25__61cc738c
upstream     : malicious. Sends a response whose header block contains a bare LF, so one header
               line carries the next header inside its value.

The proxy parses the upstream head by splitting on CRLF only, so it treats the smuggled text as
part of the previous value, and relays it unchanged. A downstream parser that also accepts bare
LF splits on LF instead and sees a different header set. The same bytes become a different
framing decision depending on which parser reads them.

--- A: smuggled CL behind TE
client received: b'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nx: a\nContent-Length: 2\r\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n2\r\nOK\r\n0\r\n\r\n'
    CRLF-strict parser: 3 fields, CL=0, TE=1
    LF-lenient parser : 4 fields, CL=1, TE=1

--- B: smuggled duplicate CL
client received: b'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nx: a\nContent-Length: 999\r\nContent-Length: 2\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\nOK'
    CRLF-strict parser: 3 fields, CL=1, TE=0
    LF-lenient parser : 4 fields, CL=2, TE=0

--- C: control, all CRLF
client received: b'HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\r\nContent-Length: 2\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n2\r'
    CRLF-strict parser: 3 fields, CL=1, TE=1
    LF-lenient parser : 3 fields, CL=1, TE=1

